# Metal Ions Confined in Periodic Pores of MOFs to Embed Single‐Metal Atoms within Hierarchically Porous Carbon Nanoflowers for High‐Performance Electromagnetic Wave Absorption

## abstract
Nanocarbons with single‐metal atoms (M‐SAs) have displayed considerable potential in various fields of application due to high free energy of M‐SAs and strong metal‐support interaction. However, the uniform dispersion of M‐SAs within the whole carbon matrix still remains a great challenge. Herein, Ni‐SAs are uniformly dispersed within hierarchically porous carbon nanoflowers (Ni‐SA/HPCF) via a spatial confinement of Ni ions within the periodic pores in metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) with a subsequent carbonization process. The Ni‐SA/HPCF with abundant mesopores and an ultrahigh surface area (1137.2 m2 g−1) exhibits unexpected electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorption property with a minimal reflection loss of –53.2 dB and an effective absorption bandwidth of 5.0 GHz, while the filler ratio in the matrix is merely 10 wt.%. Density functional theory calculations and experimental results reveal that the uniformly dispersed Ni‐SAs break local symmetry of the electronic structure and increase electrical conductivity of host carbon matrix, thereby enhancing the EMW absorption properties. In addition, the unique 3D hierarchical porous morphology boosts the impedance matching property, which synergistically improves the EMW absorption performance of the Ni‐SA/HPCF. This study provides an efficient approach to uniformly disperse M‐SAs within hierarchically porous nanocarbons for EMW absorption and other potential applications.

Nanocarbons with single-metal atoms (M-SAs) have displayed considerable potential in various fields of application due to high free energy of M-SAs and strong metal-support interaction. However, the uniform dispersion of M-SAs within the whole carbon matrix still remains a great challenge. Herein, Ni-SAs are uniformly dispersed within hierarchically porous carbon nanoflowers (Ni-SA/HPCF) via a spatial confinement of Ni ions within the periodic pores in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with a subsequent carbonization process. The Ni-SA/HPCF with abundant mesopores and an ultrahigh surface area $(7737.2\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1})$ exhibits unexpected electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorption property with a minimal reflection loss of –53.2 dB and an effective absorption bandwidth of $5.0\mathsf{C H z}$ , while the filler ratio in the matrix is merely $10w t.\%$ . Density functional theory calculations and experimental results reveal that the uniformly dispersed Ni-SAs break local symmetry of the electronic structure and increase electrical conductivity of host carbon matrix, thereby enhancing the EMW absorption properties. In addition, the unique 3D hierarchical porous morphology boosts the impedance matching property, which synergistically improves the EMW absorption performance of the Ni-SA/HPCF. This study provides an efficient approach to uniformly disperse M-SAs within hierarchically porous nanocarbons for EMW absorption and other potential applications.
## 1. Introduction
The flourishing development of highfrequency communication technology has inevitably given rise to electromagnetic radiation and electromagnetic pollution, which cause serious harm to the normal operation of precision devices and human health.[1,2] Hence, the production of highefficiency electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorbing materials for suppressing the negative effects of the electromagnetic radiation has become an urgent task. To date, the EMW absorbing materials are not only required to have strong absorption properties, but also to be lightweight and ensure their low loading amount in the dielectric matrix.[3] In this regard, 3D hierarchically porous carbon nanostructures (3D HPCs), featuring adjustable porous structure, large specific surface area, plenteous propagation paths, and high conductivity, have aroused considerable interest among the researchers.[4–7] The high electric conductivity of the 3D HPCs makes electrons migrate more easily compared to the counterparts with a low conductivity, which can improve the conduction loss for the EMW absorption capacity to some extent. The multiple levels of porosity can allow more incident EMWs to enter the interior of the 3D HPCs, thereby boosting their impedance matching characteristics. In the meanwhile, the defect sites are formed around the pore edges, which can serve as the polarization sites to increase the polarization losses of 3D HPCs and improve the EMW absorption performance.[8–10] Furthermore, the large pore volume endows the 3D HPCs with a low mass density, which benefits to the construction of lightweight EMW absorbers.[11] Nevertheless, a further improvement of the dielectric losses in 3D HPCs with the aim of enhancing the EMW absorption performance of related materials still faces a huge challenge due to a lack of sufficient polarizable centers.
Recently, single-metal atoms (M-SAs) doped nanocarbons $\left(\mathrm{M}{-}\mathrm{N_{x}}\mathrm{Cs}\right)$ have attracted much attention of the scholars due to the strong metal-support interaction and extremely high surface free energy of isolated metal atoms.[12–14] In particular, because of different electronegativities of isolated metal atoms and adjacent non-metal atoms, the electrons are transferred from M-SAs to non-metal atoms, enabling one to tune the electronic properties of the host nanocarbons. On the one hand, the charge redistribution improves the electrical conductivity of the host nanocarbons, thereby increasing the conduction loss.[13] On the other hand, the M-SAs can be regarded as the polarization centers, which produce oscillations under the external electromagnetic field, thus enhancing the polarization loss.[14] Consequently, nanocarbons with embedded M-SAs exhibit the outstanding EMW absorption characteristics. For example, the minimal reflection loss and effective absorption bandwidth of the Mn-SAs doped honeycomb-like carbon nanosheets in work could reach $-46.2$ dB and $4.7\:\mathrm{GHz}$ , respectively.[13] Therefore, the incorporation of M-SAs into 3D HPCs enables one to achieve synchronously the efficient EMW absorption and lightweight properties. However, studies on the EMW absorption characteristics of 3D HPCs containing M-SAs have rarely been reported. Furthermore, the uniform dispersion of M-SAs in the whole HPCs matrix with a specific surface area larger than $1000{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ still remains a challenge.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are porous crystal frameworks formed by bridging metal atoms and organic linkers, can serve as the hard templates to prepare $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cs}$ materials.[15–18] Although various $\mathrm{{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{{N}_{x}\mathrm{{Cs}}}}$ composites have been fabricated using MOFs as precursors so far, the original porous features of MOFs cannot be retained well due to the structural collapse during the high-temperature pyrolysis process. Therefore, the development of an efficient strategy to embed M-SAs into 3D HPCs with a large surface area is of significant importance for practical applications. Herein, a spatial confinement strategy is developed to synthesize Ni-SAs that embedded in the N-doped hierarchically porous flower-like structure (Ni-SA/ HPCF) derived from MOFs, in which the Ni-SAs were immobilized by N atoms. The strategy not only ensures the uniform distribution of Ni-SAs coordinated with N atoms in the whole HPCF matrix, but also endows the Ni-SA/HPCF system with a very large specific surface area and open porous structure. The specific surface area and the pore volume of the Ni-SA/HPCF are found to be up to $1137.2\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.58\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Combining the advantages of Ni-SAs with a unique structure of HPCF, the Ni-SA/HPCF composite exhibits excellent EMW absorption performances at a minimal reflection loss $(R L_{\mathrm{min}})$ of $-53.2~\mathrm{dB}$ and an effective absorption bandwidth $(\mathrm{EAB}_{10})$ of $5.0\mathrm{GHz}$ . It is noteworthy that the above characteristics could be attained at the relatively low filler content in the paraffin matrix (10  wt. $\%$ ). Furthermore, the EMW absorption mechanism of the Ni-SA/HPCF composite is demonstrated through density functional theory (DFT) calculations and experimental analysis.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The synthesis process of Ni-SA/HPCF systems is schematically illustrated in Figure 1a (more details about the synthesis process can be found in the Experimental section). An aluminum-based MOF $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ ) was selected as a host precursor in this work due to its fabulously large pore size $(\approx3.0\ \mathrm{nm})$ and high specific surface area $(2097\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ; for more details, please refer to Figure  S1a Supporting Information). First, dicyandiamide (DCD) and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ were dissolved in N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and then dropwise added into the $n$ -hexane containing $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ under vigorous stirring. The large inner surface area of the hydrophilic $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ allowed a small amount of the hydrophilic DMF solution with DCD and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ to diffuse into the hydrophilic pores of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ that was suspended in a high amount of the dry $n$ -hexane under the capillary force. During the diffusion process, the deposition of the guest precursors on the outer surface of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was greatly limited due to the fact that the inner surface area of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exceeded the outer surface area.[19] Thus, the guests could be distributed uniformly in the pores of the pristine $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{NiDCD}@\mathrm{ML}\mathrm{-}101\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ ). The decreased pore volume and BET surface area of the $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{NiDCD}@\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ specimen $(0.63~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1041~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively) relative to those of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ system $(1.52~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $2097~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ confirmed that the guest precursors were efficiently diffused in the pores of the latter one (Figure S1 and Table S1, Supporting Information). The pyrolysis of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{NiDCD}@\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ and subsequent HF etching enabled one to obtain a Ni-SA/HPCF structure. During the pyrolysis, DCD was decomposed into N-containing species, while Ni ions were reduced to metallic Ni species or bonded with $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms within the interior pore spaces of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ structure, which could generate plenty of heteroatoms such as $\mathrm{~N~}$ dopants and Ni-SAs in MOF-derived porous carbons. Because the periodic pores in the MOFs served as nanoreactors, the decomposition of the DCD and the reduction reactions of Ni ions occurred in the confined spaces, leading to the uniform distribution of Ni-SAs throughout the N-doped carbon skeleton. In addition, the decomposition of the DCD and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ released a large amount of gases upon the pyrolysis, resulting in the formation of a unique hierarchical nanoflower-like structure with abundant mesopores. Finally, the produced Al-species and Ni nanoparticles were removed through the HF etching, and Ni-SAs remained in the N-doped carbon skeleton.
The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images reveal a particle-like morphology of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with a relatively smooth surface (Figure  S2, Supporting Information). In contrast, the Ni-SA/HPCF specimen exhibits an interesting porous nanoflower-like structure, which is assembled with interconnected 8-nm-thick carbon nanosheets (Figure  1b; Figure  S3, Supporting Information). The magnified SEM images show that the size of the open pores between the adjacent nanosheets is ${\approx}35~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S3, Supporting Information). To uncover the formation mechanism, different control samples were fabricated, including CS, HPCF, and Ni-SA/CS after the pyrolysis of pure $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ confined with either DCD or $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions, respectively. According to the SEM images,
CS and Ni-SA/CS have the particle-like morphologies, while HPCF possesses a nanoflower-like structure similar to that of Ni-SA/HPCF (Figures  S4–S6, Supporting Information). Thus, the confined DCD within the periodic pores of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ played a key role in the formation of nanoflower-like samples with a 3D HPC feature. The HPCF and Ni-SA/HPCF samples with unique morphologies are expected to have the large specific surface areas and abundant pore structures. Figure  1c and Figure  S7, (Supporting Information), show the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of Ni-SA/HPCF and other control samples. The type-IV isotherms are presented for all samples, indicating that their mesoporous features.[20] The pore sizes in Ni-SA/HPCF and HPCF samples are mainly centered at ${\approx}2.3$ and $10.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in the insets of Figure 1c and Figure  S7b, (Supporting Information). As expected, the BET surface areas of Ni-SA/HPCF and HPCF were 1137.2 and $1079.1\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, exceeding those of CS $(550~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and Ni-SA/CS $(620~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Furthermore, the pore volumes of Ni-SA/HPCF and HPCF (1.58 and $1.51~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively) were much greater than those of CS $(0.71\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and Ni-SA/ CS $(0.68\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Notably, the surface area of the Ni-SA/HPCF system was also larger than those of most previously reported HPC nanostructures (Table  S1, Supporting Information). This means that Ni-SA/HPCF featuring the large specific surface area and abundant pores can not only improve the impedance matching characteristics, but also provide more edges as the polarization centers to increase the polarization loss, which is conducive to EMW absorption capability of the material.[21,22] Furthermore, the large pore volume endows Ni-SA/HPCF with an ultralight feature for EMW absorption.
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images confirm the porous structure of the Ni-SA/HPCF sample without any metal nanoparticles observed (Figure  1d; Figure  S8, Supporting Information). Moreover, scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) and energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDX) mappings images reveal the uniform distribution of C, N, and Ni elements throughout the nanosheets, suggesting the presence of atomically dispersed Ni atoms in the Ni-SA/HPCF (Figure  1e). According to the aberration-corrected HAADF-STEM (AC-HAADF-STEM) data, isolated bright dots in the carbon skeleton are indexed to heavy Ni-SAs in the Ni-SA/HPCF (Figure  1f).[23] Besides, the TEM images exhibit the hierarchical porous structure of the HPCF sample, similar to that of Ni-SA/HPCF (Figure  S5b, Supporting Information). In contrast, the CS and Ni-SA/CS samples possess quite different structural features (Figures  S4b and S6b, Supporting Information), leading to a decrease in their BET surface areas relative to those of Ni-SA/HPCF and HPCF. The EDX mappings images reveal that the HPCF and CS samples contain C and N elements (Figures S4c and S5c, Supporting Information). Notably, no metal nanoparticles were also detected in the Ni-SA/CS sample, while the uniformly distributed C, N, and Ni elements indicated the existence of Ni-SAs in the sample (Figure  S6c, Supporting Information). These results mean that the DCD and Ni ions confined within the periodic pores of the MOFs not only play an important role in the formation of the 3D unique nanoflower-like Ni-SA/HPCF structure, but also enable Ni-SAs to be uniformly embedded in the N-doped carbon skeleton.
The XRD data of Ni-SA/HPCF and control samples are quite similar, in which two broad peaks centered at ${\approx}25^{\circ}$ and $44^{\circ}$ correspond to the (002) and (101) planes of graphitized carbon, respectively. The diffraction peaks from the metallic Ni nanoparticles/clusters are not detected (Figure 2a), further revealing that the metallic components in Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS are Ni-SAs. The Raman spectra (Figure  2b) of Ni-SA/HPCF and control samples can be deconvoluted into four types of carbon configurations at 1350 $\mathrm{(D_{1})}$ , 1200 $\left(\mathrm{D}_{2}\right)$ , 1500 $\left(\mathrm{D}_{3}\right)$ , and
1592 (G) $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively.[24] The $\mathsf{D}_{1}$ band and G band in the Raman spectra correspond to the defects and disorders in the carbon lattice and the in-plane tangential stretching of the $\mathsf{C}^{\mathrm{-C}}$ bonds in $\displaystyle\mathtt{s p}^{2}$ carbons, respectively.[24] The $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ band is associated with carbon atoms around the grain boundaries of crystalline graphene, regarded as edge defects.[24] The ${\bf D}_{3}$ band can be ascribed to the amorphous $\displaystyle\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ carbon.[25] Generally, the defect degree of carbon can be evaluated by the ratio of the relative integrated area of the $\mathsf{D}_{1}$ to that of the G band.[25] The $I_{\mathrm{D1}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ intensity ratios of the Ni-SA/HPCF (1.82), Ni-SA/CS (1.60), and HPCF (1.57) specimens exceed that of the CS sample (1.34), which implies that the presence of Ni-SAs and abundant pores facilitates the creation of more defective sites in the carbon skeleton. These defective sites can serve as the polarization centers, which are conducive to enhancement of polarization loss and thereby improve the EMW absorption performance of samples.[26] According to the survey X-ray photoelectron (XPS) spectra, the Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS samples contain Ni, C, N, and O elements, while C, N, and O signals are observed in the XPS spectra of the HPCF and CS materials (Figure S9, Supporting Information). The high-resolution C 1s XPS spectra of Ni-SA/HPCF and control samples were deconvoluted into three characteristics peaks at 288.7, 285.3, and $284.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ which corresponded to ${\mathrm{O-C=O}}$ , CN, and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ groups, respectively (Figure  S10, Supporting Information).[27] The peaks located at 398.5, 400.3, 401.3, and $403.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the high-resolution $\mathrm{~N~}$ 1s XPS spectra of Ni-SA/HPCF and control samples are attributed to pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, graphitic N, and N–oxide, respectively (Figure 2d), thereby confirming that $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms have been successfully doped into the carbon matrix.[28] Besides, an additional peak at $399.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ appears in the N 1s XPS spectra of Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS, which is ascribed to $\mathrm{Ni-N_{x}}$ species,[29] further confirming the existence of Ni-SAs coordinated with N atoms in the two samples (Figure 2d). The total $\mathrm{~N~}$ contents in Ni-SA/HPCF, Ni-SA/CS, HPCF, and CS are 6.2, 4.9, 5.2, and $3.2\ \mathrm{at.}\%$ , respectively (Figure  2c). The higher N concentrations in the Ni-SA/HPCF and HPCF are attributed to the usage of DCD during the synthesis processes. Previous reports have proved that pyridinic N and pyrrolic N benefit to the enhancement of the polarization losses, whereas graphitic N contributes to the increase in the conduction losses.[28] Thus, the high contents of these N species exert a positive influence on the EMW absorption properties of the Ni-SA/HPCF. In the high-resolution Ni 2p spectra of the Ni-SA/HPCF and $\mathrm{Ni}{\cdot}\mathrm{SA}/\$ CS samples (Figure 2e), the Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ peaks located at 855.6 and $873.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ with two shakeup satellites at 861.3 and $879.7\ \mathrm{eV};$ , respectively, can be indexed to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ species, while the peaks that correspond to the metallic Ni species are absent.[29] According to the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES), the contents of the Ni species in the Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS samples are 1.12 and $0.93\mathrm{wt.}\%$ , respectively (Figure 2c). Higher Ni content in the Ni-SA/HPCF originates from its higher N content since Ni-SAs through the strong coordination interactions between the $d$ orbitals of the metal atoms and the lone pair electrons of the N atom in the N-doped carbon matrix.[30] In addition, peaks near $73\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the survey XPS spectra originate from the residual Al species in the samples (Figure  S9, Supporting Information). The contents of Al atoms in the Ni-SA/HPCF, Ni-SA/CS, HPCF, and CS are ${\approx}0.10$ wt. $\%$ determined by ICP-OES, much lower than those of Ni atoms in Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS.
The Ni-SA/HPCF samples with atomically dispersed Ni-SAs in the 3D HPCF structure were successfully prepared through the space confinement strategy and the immobilization of N atoms followed by the pyrolysis and acid etching. The as-prepared Ni-SA/HPCF exhibits a 3D porous open nanoflower-like morphology with an extremely large surface area of $1137.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-\bar{1}}$ . Importantly, the nanoflowers are assembled with interconnected carbon nanosheets with abundant defects and heteroatom dopants. These unique features of the Ni-SA/ HPCF composite imply its potential in the EMW absorption application fields. To investigate the EMW absorption properties of Ni-SA/HPCF and control samples, the electromagnetic parameters (complex permittivity $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}=\varepsilon^{\prime}-j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime})$ and permeability $(\mu_{\mathrm{r}}=\mu^{\prime}-j\mu^{\prime\prime}))$ were measured with the same filler loading of $10\mathrm{wt.\%}$ in the paraffin wax within a frequency range of $2-18\operatorname{GHz}$ . Among these parameters, the real parts $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ represent the storage abilities of the electric and magnetic energies, respectively, while the imaginary parts $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ are related to the dissipation abilities of these energies.[33] The dielectric loss tangent (tan $\delta_{\varepsilon}=\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime})$ ) and magnetic loss tangent (tan $\delta_{\mathrm{\mu}}=\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime})$ are adopted to evaluate the EMW losses of the absorbers.[34] As shown in Figure 3a,b, the permittivity values of all the samples decrease with an increase in the frequency due to the frequency dispersion effect. The $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ values decrease from 10.1 to 6.8 for Ni-SA/HPCF, from 7.6 to 5.7 for Ni-SA/CS, from 5.6 to 4.1 for HPCF, and from 5.0 to 3.8 for CS. The $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values vary from 5.3 to 2.5 for Ni-SA/HPCF, from 2.9 to 1.4 for Ni-SA/CS, from 2.0 to 1.3 for HPCF, and from 1.4 to 0.9 for CS, showing a similar descending tendency to that of the real parts. It is found that $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values of the carbon matrix drastically increase upon the introduction of Ni-SAs, indicating that the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ moieties can regulate the permittivity of the Ni-SA-containing samples. The higher tan $\delta_{\varepsilon}$ values of the Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS demonstrate that the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ moieties also enhance the dielectric loss property of the host carbon matrix, as compared to HPCF and CS (Figure  3c). Figure  S11, (Supporting Information), depicts the changes in permeability with the frequency of the samples. The $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ values are almost 1 and 0, respectively, over the range of $2-18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ , which is due to the lack of the magnetic loss factors in these samples, suggesting their weak magnetic loss performance.[35] Thus, the EMW absorption properties of the mentioned materials are mainly determined by their dielectric losses.
The origin of the dielectric losses in these samples was first analyzed by the Cole-Cole formula.[36] The semicircles and straight lines are apparent in the Cole-Cole plots, indicating both polarization relaxation loss and conduction loss contribute to the dielectric losses of all the samples (Figure  S12, Supporting Information).[37] Generally, conduction loss $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}^{\prime\prime})$ can be estimated by the conductivity of the material $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}^{\prime\prime}=\sigma/\omega\varepsilon_{0})$ .[38] According to the Hall-effect measurements, the conductivities of the Ni-SA/HPCF, Ni-SA/CS, HPCF, and CS specimens are 3.39, 2.51, 1.16, and $0.58\mathrm{~S~m~}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure  3d). Thus, the larger conductivities of the Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/ CS samples enhance their conduction losses, as compared to those of HPCF and CS (Figure  3e). Thus, the introduction of Ni-SAs in the samples can improve their loss properties toward the EMW. The polarization relaxation losses $(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{p}}^{\ \prime\prime}=\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}^{\ \prime\prime})$ can be calculated based on the $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}^{\prime\prime}$ data. As shown in Figure 3f, the polarization relaxation losses of the samples follow the order of $\mathrm{Ni{-}S A/H P C F>N i{-}S A/C S>H P C F>C S}$ , indicating that the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ moieties can effectively enhance the polarization loss of the host carbon matrix. Therefore, based on the experimental results, the rise in the dielectric loss values of Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS is caused by the enhancement in both the conduction and polarization losses upon the introduction of Ni-SAs.
To gain in-depth insight into the relationships between Ni-SAs and dielectric loss property of the Ni-SA/HPCF sample, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed.
The NC and $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ models for the HPCF and Ni-SA/HPCF samples were built for theoretical calculations (Figure  4a,b). The energy band structure and the corresponding density of states (DOS) were plotted to analyze the effect of the Ni-SAs on the electronic properties of the host carbon matrix. The conductivity of a material is closely related to the bandgap width, that is, the larger the bandgap is, the lower the conductivity is, and vice versa. Therefore, the conductivity of the material can be discussed in terms of its energy band structure. Figure  4c depicts the energy band structures of the models for the NC and $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ models along the Γ-M-K-Γ line. The NC exhibits a semiconducting property with a small bandgap of $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}.$ In contrast, $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ demonstrates a nearly metallic behavior with a negligible bandgap of $0.009\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the K point. The lower bandgap of the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ system indicates its higher electrical conductivity, which is consistent with the experimental results. Figure  4d displays the DOSs of the NC and $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ models.
It can be found that the total DOS of the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ structure is larger than that of the NC one due to the contribution from the $3d$ orbitals of Ni-SAs near the Fermi level, suggesting the higher electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ . An increase in the conductivity of the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ model improves its conduction loss, which coincides with the results shown in Figure  3e. The Mulliken charges and charge density differences were further calculated to analyze the effect of Ni-SAs on the electronic structure of the host carbon matrix. In the NC model, the estimated Mulliken charges of N and C are $-0.32$ and 0.17, respectively, while the charges of the Ni, N, and C atoms in the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ model are 1.20, –0.45, and 0.16, respectively. Thus, the introduction of Ni-SAs induces the emergence of more negative charges from the adjacent N atoms, resulting in the symmetry breaking of the local microstructure and the formation of additional electrical dipoles.[39] The oscillations of these dipoles under the external electromagnetic field can enhance the polarization losses of the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ system. The charge density difference enables one to better visualize the charge interaction between the adjacent atoms. Figure  4e displays the NC and $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ model structure and their corresponding charge density differences. In the NC model, the electrons are transferred from the C (yellow) atom to the adjacent N atom (pale blue), and from the Ni atom (yellow) to the adjacent C or N atom (pale blue) in the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ model. Thus, the charge density difference analysis further confirms that the charge redistribution and the Ni-SAs induce the formation of the additional electrical dipoles. To provide the in-depth understanding of the origin of the enhanced polarization loss caused by Ni-SAs, the dipole moments of the NC and $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ models were calculated. The results show that the dipole moment of the $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}C}$ system (6.9 Debye) exceeds that of NC (5.2 Debye) (Figure  4f). The larger dipole moment can induce the stronger polarization loss, further confirming that the introduction of Ni-SAs can enhance the polarization loss of the host carbon matrix. Based on the above DFT calculations, the regulation of the DOSs and band structures in Ni-SA/ HPCF and Ni-SA/CS by Ni-SAs contributes to the enhancement in the conduction losses, while the symmetry breaking of the local electronic structure and the additional electric dipoles induced by Ni-SAs are responsible for the increase in the polarization losses. In addition, the HPCF exhibits an increase in the dielectric loss compared to CS with a low surface area, indicating that the uniform hierarchical porous feature and the large surface area of the HPCF have the positive effects on the dielectric loss. The results also demonstrate that combining the single metal atoms and the unique 3D HPC structure with an extremely large surface area facilitate the improvement of the dielectric loss performance of the whole material. However, the dielectric loss of the HPCF with the greater surface area is inferior to that of Ni-SA/CS with the smaller surface area, meaning that the Ni-SAs play a decisive role in the enhancement of the dielectric loss.
The increased dielectric performances of Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/CS reveal that their EMW absorption properties might have been enhanced as well. The EMW absorption characteristics of the samples were first evaluated by considering the reflection loss (RL) gained based on the transmission line theory.[40] Figure  5a–d shows the 2D and 3D reflection loss curves of the CS, HPCF, Ni-SA/CS, and Ni-SA/HPCF samples with the matching thicknesses in the range of $1.5\mathrm{~-~}5.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ and the frequencies of $2\textrm{--}18\textrm{G H z}$ . The minimal reflection loss $(R L_{\mathrm{min}})$ value of the CS sample did not reach $^{-10}$ dB, indicating the bad EMW absorption property (Figure  5a-1). Compared with CS, the $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ values of HPCF almost reached −10 dB at the matching thickness of $1.5-5.0\:\mathrm{mm}$ (Figure 5b-1). The EMW absorption properties of Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/ CS increased more obviously. For example, the $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ value of Ni-SA/CS at $6.3\ \mathrm{GHz}$ is $-17.5$ dB at the thickness of $5.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ (Figure  5c-1), whereas that of Ni-SA/HPCF at $8.6~\mathrm{GHz}$ has increased to $-53.2$ dB at even a smaller thickness of $3.5~\mathrm{mm}$ (Figure 5d-1). The effective absorption bandwidth $\mathrm{\DeltaEAB_{10}}$ , with the frequency range corresponding to $R L{<}{-}10$ dB) is another important factor for the practical application of an absorber.[41] As shown in Figure  5e, the $\mathrm{EAB}_{10}$ values of Ni-SA/HPCF $(5.0\mathrm{GHz})$ and Ni-SA/CS $(2.8~\mathrm{GHz})$ are also greater than those of HPCF $(<1\mathrm{GHz})$ and CS $(0\mathrm{\GHz})$ , suggesting that the introduction of Ni-SAs can increase the EMW absorption properties of the host carbon matrix. Notably, even if the thickness is as small as $1.7~\mathrm{mm}$ , the RL value of Ni-SA/HPCF is still at the level of $-30$ dB (Figure  5f ), indicating that up to $99.9\%$ of the incident EMW can be effectively absorbed by the sample.[42] To compare the EMW absorption property of the Ni-SA/HPCF structure with other reported absorbers, the radar chart based on the four parameters (minimal reflection loss, matching thickness, filler loading, and effective absorption bandwidth) is plotted in Figure 5g. The better absorber should possess a low minimal reflection loss, a small thickness, a low filler loading, and a broad effective absorption bandwidth. Thus, in the radar chart, the larger the area of the 2D quadrilateral enclosed by the four points, the higher the comprehensive EMW absorption performance the absorber. As shown in Figure  5g, the Ni-SA/HPCF sample has a much larger area compared to those of the reported carbon-based absorbers,[44–51] further indicating its potential application in the EMW absorption field (Table S3, Supporting Information).
Besides, the EMW absorption property of an absorber are also relevant to its impedance matching characteristics, which are usually evaluated by the degree of impedance matching $(M_{\mathrm{Z}}=2Z_{\mathrm{in}}{}^{\prime}/(|Z_{\mathrm{in}}|^{2}+1))$ . If the $M_{\mathrm{Z}}$ value of the absorber is equal or close to 1, it means the ideal impedance matching characteristics.[43] As seen in Figure  6a, the $M_{\mathrm{Z}}$ values of the $\mathrm{Ni}{\cdot}\mathrm{SA}/\$ HPCF composite are much closer to 1 than those of other control samples over a range of $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{~-~}}18\ensuremath{\mathrm{~GHz}}$ , indicating that the former possesses better impedance matching performance. The improvement in the impedance matching parameters of Ni-SA/HPCF relative to those of Ni-SA/CS means that the unique porous nanoflower structure is also conducive to the enhancement of the ability of a material to attenuate the electromagnetic energy. In addition, the attenuation constant $(\alpha)$ was also calculated to evaluate the attenuation ability of the samples.[52] Figure 6b clearly shows that the Ni-SA/HPCF specimen has the largest $\alpha$ value (45 − 243) compared with Ni-SA/ CS (26 − 173), HPCF (30 − 152), and CS (12 − 86), meaning that Ni-SA/HPCF possesses the strongest EMW attenuation ability among all samples in this study. Based on the experimental results, the Ni-SA/HPCF specimen demonstrates the lowest $R L_{\mathrm{min:}}$ , the broadest $\mathrm{EAB}_{10}$ , and the largest $\alpha$ value as well as the best impedance matching characteristic (Figure  6c), revealing the importance of introducing Ni-SAs into the 3D HPC with a large surface area with the aim of improving EMW absorption (Figure 6d).
To study the effect of carbonization temperature on the EMW absorption properties, we prepared two additional samples at 700 and $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The samples obtained at 700, 800, and $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were denoted as Ni-SA/HPCF-700, Ni-SA/HPCF-800, and Ni-SA/HPCF-900, respectively. XRD and XPS measurement indicate that the Ni-SA/HPCF-700 and Ni-SA/HPCF-900 have almost the same phase compositions and the valence states of Ni-SAs as those of the Ni-SA/HPCF-800 (Figures S13 and S14, Supporting Information). SEM and TEM images indicate that the Ni-SA/HPCF-700 and Ni-SA/HPCF-900 also exhibit nanoflower-like structures (Figure  S15, Supporting Information).
However, the porous structure of the Ni-SA/HPCF-900 collapses and shrinks slightly (Figure  S15c, Supporting Information). $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms indicate that the BET surface area of Ni-SA/HPCF-700 and Ni-SA/HPCF-900 are 1017 and $995~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are slightly lower than that of Ni-SA/HPCF-800 (Figure  S16, Supporting Information). Figure  S17 (Supporting Information) shows that the Ni-SA/HPCF-700 and the Ni-SA/HPCF-900 have lower EMW absorption performances than that of the Ni-SA/HPCF-800. Therefore, the optimal carbonization temperature of the Ni-SA/ HPCF sample is $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in this work. In addition, the EMW absorption performances of the Ni-SA/HPCF sample contained different content of Ni-SAs are also investigated. The content of Ni-SAs in the Ni-SA/HPCF sample was controlled by changing the amount of used metal salts. The samples obtained with the amount of metal salts of 10, 50, 100, and $150~\mathrm{mg}$ were named as Ni-SA/HPCF-1, Ni-SA/HPCF-2, Ni-SA/HPCF-3, and Ni-SA/ HPCF-4, respectively. The metallic Ni nanoparticles/clusters were not detected in the Ni-SA/HPCF-1, Ni-SA/HPCF-2, and Ni-SA/HPCF-4 by the XRD (Figure  S18, Supporting Information). SEM images reveal nanoflower-like structures of these samples (Figure S19, Supporting Information). ICP-OES measurement indicates that the contents of the Ni species in the Ni-SA/HPCF-1, Ni-SA/HPCF-2, and Ni-SA/HPCF-4 are ${\approx}0.23$ , 0.41, and $1.13~\mathrm{wt.}\%$ , respectively. It can be found that when the amount of $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ exceeds $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ , the content of Ni-SAs is almost unchanged due to upper limit of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions confined in the pores of MOFs. The 2D reflection loss curves demonstrate that the EMW absorption property of the samples follows the order of Ni-SA/HPCF-1 $<$ Ni-SA/HPCF- $2<$ Ni-SA/HPCF-3 ∼ Ni-SA/HPCF-4 according to the $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ and $\mathrm{EAB}_{10}$ values (Figure  5d; Figure  S20, Supporting Information). Thus, the EMW absorption property of the Ni-SA/HPCF is increased with the increase of the content of Ni-SAs.
According to the above theoretical calculations and experimental results, the enhanced EMW absorption performance of the Ni-SA/HPCF material is directly correlated with its 3D hierarchical porous morphology and uniform dispersion of
Ni-SAs as well as large surface area (Figure  7). i) DFT calculations demonstrate (Figure  4) that the uniformly dispersed Ni-SAs lower the bandgap and increase the DOS in the Ni-SA/ HPCF sample compared to the counterparts without Ni-SAs, which increases the conduction loss behavior of the absorber. In the meanwhile, the incorporation of Ni-SAs induces the charge redistribution and creates the electrical dipoles, which significantly enhance the polarization loss.[53] The experimental results (Figures 3 and 5) show that the Ni-SA/HPCF and Ni-SA/ CS exhibit better dielectric loss and EMW absorption properties than other samples without Ni-SAs, suggesting that Ni-SAs play a main important role in the enhanced properties, also evidenced by the fact that the sample contained high content of Ni-SAs exhibits better EMW absorption property. ii) On the other hand, Ni-SA/HPCF and HPCF with higher pore volumes show better absorption property than Ni-SA/CS and CS with lower pore volumes, respectively. Thus, the abundant porosity also plays a positive effect on the EMW absorption property. The 3D hierarchical nanoflowers with abundant mesopores can provide sufficient free spaces for improving the impedance matching that facilitated the EMW absorption property. In addition, the large pore volume of the samples can increase their volume fractions in the paraffin matrix at the same filler loading.[54] The higher volume fraction can boost the multiple scattering of the incident EMW inside the absorbing film and promote the formation of conductive networks.[54] Notably, the influence of porosity discussed above was based on a series of materials with different chemical properties. To further elucidate the effect of porosity, we calculated the dipole moments of the materials with similar chemical properties. We first considered the N-doped graphene with and without porosity. One model for NG without porosity (NG), and three models for NG with porosity $(\mathrm{NG-D}_{1}$ with one C atom missing, $\mathsf{N G}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{D}_{2}$ with two C atoms missing, and $\mathsf{N G}-\mathsf{D}_{3}$ with three C atoms missing) were built (Figure  S21a, Supporting Information). The calculated results show that the dipole moment of the models follows the order of $\mathrm{NG}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{D}_{3}>\mathrm{NG}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{D}_{2}>\mathrm{NG}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{D}_{1}>\mathrm{NG}$ , suggesting that the porosity can improve the dielectric loss property of NG and the enhancement becomes more obvious as the number of the pores is increased (Figure  S21b, Supporting Information). Second, we considered the Ni-SAs-contained N-doped graphene with and without porosity. One model for the material without porosity $(\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}-G})$ , and three models for the materials with porosity $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{(\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{-N}_{4}\mathrm{-D}_{1}}}&{{}}\end{array}$ , $\mathrm{Ni-N}_{4}{\mathrm{-D}}_{2}$ , and $N_{1-}\mathrm{N}_{4}{-}\mathrm{D}_{3}$ ) were built (Figure  S22a, Supporting Information). These models containing Ni-SAs show the same variation trend of the dipole moment as that of the models based on NG (Figure  S22b, Supporting Information). Thus, the samples with large pore volume and high surface area show enhanced EMW absorption property. iii) The Ni-SA/HPCF with more defects shows superior EMW absorption property to that of the Ni-SA/CS. Thus, the defects in the samples can promote the EMW absorption property. In fact, the defects can serve as the polarization centers to increase dielectric loss ability, thus increasing the EMW absorption performance of the composite.[55] The Ni-SA/ CS has comparable defects to HPCF; however, the former shows better EMW absorption property, suggesting that the defects play an assistant role in enhancing EMW absorption performance rather than determining factors. In addition, the CS and HPCF samples without Ni atoms exhibit lower EMW absorption properties than Ni-SA/CS and Ni-SA/HPCF samples, indicating the residual Al atoms in these samples do not play a crucial role in their EMW absorption properties.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a spatially confined strategy was developed to confine Ni-SAs within the hierarchical porous carbon nanoflowers using the MOFs as precursors. Benefiting from the unique hierarchical porous feature and uniformly dispersed Ni-SAs, the resultant Ni-SA/HPCF structure exhibited excellent EMW absorption performance with the minimal RL value of $-53.2$ dB and the effective absorption bandwidth of $5.0\mathrm{GHz}$ at a filler content of merely $10\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ . These values were superior to those of counterparts without Ni-SAs and most reported carbon-based absorbers. The DFT calculations and experimental data demonstrated that the increase in the EMW absorption properties of the Ni-SA/HPCF specimen was attributed to the enhanced polarization and conduction losses owing to the synergistic effect of the incorporated Ni-SAs and the hierarchical morphology. Therefore, the proposed spatial confinement approach opens up new prospects for developing high-efficiency EMW absorbing materials.
